  "course_description": "May be repeated for credit when topic varies. Pre/co-requisites vary with topic.",
  "course_notes": "Human-AI Interaction: Foundations and Frontiers Prerequisite: CS 253 Department permission required, please email bnflood@emory.edu What if the key to designing the future of human-AI interaction lies in understanding its past? The history of Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) is more than a record of old interfaces. It is a roadmap of ideas, breakthroughs, and failures that reveal how humans adapt to technology, and how technology adapts to us. These foundations continue to shape today's frontiers in generative AI, intelligent interfaces, ubiquitous computing, and human-AI collaboration. Many technologies we now take for granted were shaped by how researchers modeled human cognition, designed interaction techniques, built computational systems, and accounted for social context. By revisiting these foundations, we can ask critical questions about how AI systems ought to augment human capabilities and reshape human experience rather than merely accepting that they will. In this course, we will examine the foundational models, theories, and technical visions that have shaped HCI over the last 50 years, from early interactive systems to today's AI-powered systems. We will connect these ideas to current frontiers in generative AI, foundation models, sensing, immersive systems, accessibility, collaborative technologies, and intelligent agents. By tracing how foundational ideas evolve alongside new computational capabilities, students will develop the conceptual grounding needed to critically analyze, design, and evaluate the next generation of human-AI and interactive systems.",
